<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cetina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cetina is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, flowing about 100 kilometres from the Dinaric mountains to the Adriatic at Omiš. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked the western limit of the Narentine principality of Pagania, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poljica">Poljica</a> district lay between its lower course and the coast; it later formed a frontier of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cetina.jpg" alt="Cetina" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikolaj Potanin · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cetina_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Cetina has its source in the northwestern slopes of Dinara, rising from a spring at Milasevo near a small village also called Cetina, 7 kilometres north of Vrlika. It flows 101 kilometres to the Adriatic, and its basin covers 1,463 square kilometres, though the whole catchment is around 12,000 square kilometres because the river also receives water from the west Bosnian karst field by underground routes.</p><p>A large artificial lake, the Peruća Lake, begins near Vrlika. The river then passes through Sinj and Trilj, runs back westward round the mountain Mosor and enters the sea at Omiš. Its lower course begins at the Gubavica Falls, 20 kilometres from Omiš. The catchment drains mostly limestone of Cretaceous age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Cetina Valley and the narrow passage at Klis have always served as a principal trade route between the Croatian coast and the hinterland. The earliest evidence of agriculture is from the Early Neolithic in the upper valley. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> the Cetina culture, a widespread group with contacts across the Adriatic basin, became dominant. The river seems to have been a focus for the deliberate deposition of artefacts throughout prehistory, above all at its confluence with the Ruda at Trilj.</p><p>The area is closely associated with the heartland of the Delmatae. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress at Tilurium (Gardun) guarded the entrance to the valley from the south and the approach to the provincial capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the valley formed a frontier between Slavic and Late Roman power, and the area around Sinj became a heartland of the Early Croatian State. It was contested until the Ottoman conquest in the early 16th century, and then lay on the frontier between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> for 150 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Several substantial hydroelectric power plants use the steep drop of its lower course, and its water is bottled under the name Cetina. It is the most water-rich river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and is home to the only known freshwater coralline alga, Pneophyllum cetinaensis, which lives nowhere else. The canyon is a destination for rafting, canyoning, kayaking and rock climbing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cetina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Serbian armies reached Duvno, <b>Cetina</b>, the Krka and Imotski, and some Bosnian nobles submitted, but most Bosnian fortresses held out.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Stephen II Kotromanić (1322–1353) Bosnia annexed Zachlumia and the land up to the <b>Cetina</b>, and Saxon miners opened silver and lead mines at Srebrenica and Fojnica, trading through Ragusa.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-bosnia">Banate of Bosnia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Narentines were Slavs who settled the coast between the <b>Cetina</b> and Neretva rivers in the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/pagania">Pagania</a></li><li><blockquote>Poljica, a hilly district between the <b>Cetina</b> river and the coast near Split, had long enjoyed local autonomy under Croatian and Hungarian kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-poljica">Republic of Poljica</a></li></ul></section>
